from pathlib import Path
P=Path(__file__).parent/'dist'
f=P/'medical-safety.html';s=f.read_text()
def shot(label,form='Desktop'):
 return f'''<figure class="safety-shot"><div class="safety-shot-space"><span>{form.upper()} SCREENSHOT SPACE</span><b aria-hidden="true">▣</b><strong>{label}</strong><small>Add your ArcHorizon capture here</small></div><figcaption>Suggested capture: {label.lower()}.</figcaption></figure>'''
main=f'''<main class="safety-page"><section class="safety-hero"><div class="wrap safety-hero-grid"><div><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Overview</a> / <a href="features.html">Features</a> / Safety &amp; Medical Support</div><span class="eyebrow">Before release · During work · At return</span><h1>Safety belongs in the assignment.</h1><p>A hazard changes the plan only when the people assigning and carrying out the work can see it. ArcHorizon links the hazard picture, operational risk assessment, release checks, weather, and field reports to the teams and tasks they affect.</p><a class="safety-hero-link" href="#release">Follow the release workflow ↓</a></div>{shot('Safety overview with hazards, risk, and release status')}</div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap safety-rhythm"><div class="section-head"><span class="eyebrow">Operational rhythm</span><h2>Review risk at the points that matter.</h2><p class="lead">Safety information starts before a team leaves and remains part of the assignment while conditions change.</p></div><div class="safety-phase-grid"><article><span>01 / BEFORE</span><h3>Understand the exposure</h3><p>Identify hazards, relevant weather, terrain, access, and the team’s readiness. Record controls and release conditions.</p></article><article><span>02 / DURING</span><h3>Watch changing conditions</h3><p>Receive field hazard reports and weather alerts. Pass meaningful changes to operations and affected teams.</p></article><article><span>03 / AFTER</span><h3>Carry lessons forward</h3><p>Keep the safety narrative with the task, team, and incident record for follow-up assignments and reporting.</p></article></div></div></section><section class="section alt"><div class="wrap safety-map-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">Hazards with location</span><h2>Put the condition where the work is.</h2><p class="lead">A hazard can be drawn on the operational map and connected to an assignment. Its location and description give planning, operations, and field personnel a common reference.</p><div class="safety-detail-list"><div><strong>Map it</strong><span>Use a point or area for the affected location.</span></div><div><strong>Explain it</strong><span>Describe the condition and who or what it affects.</span></div><div><strong>Act on it</strong><span>Adjust the route, assignment, briefing, or resource choice.</span></div></div><a class="text-link" href="mapping.html">Explore operational mapping →</a></div>{shot('GIS map showing hazard area and affected assignment')}</div></section><section class="section safety-risk"><div class="wrap safety-risk-grid">{shot('Operational risk assessment with controls and review')}<div><span class="eyebrow">Operational risk management</span><h2>Assess the task, then document the controls.</h2><p class="lead">The risk matrix gives staff a consistent way to consider likelihood and consequence. Record the factors, proposed controls, and review decision alongside the work being released.</p><ul class="safety-check-list"><li>Evaluate the task and operating conditions.</li><li>Choose controls the team can actually apply.</li><li>Reassess when the hazard or assignment changes.</li></ul></div></div></section><section class="section alt" id="release"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="eyebrow">Ground resource release</span><h2>A deliberate check before dispatch.</h2><p class="lead">The Ground Release Officer can see whether the operational pieces are in place before a ground team moves: the assignment, the resource, the paperwork, and the conditions that govern release.</p></div><div class="release-grid"><div class="release-items"><div><span>01</span><strong>Assignment ready</strong><p>Task, supervisor, location, and hazards are clear.</p></div><div><span>02</span><strong>Team accountable</strong><p>Personnel, status, and tracking are established.</p></div><div><span>03</span><strong>Paperwork complete</strong><p>Required documents and briefings are accounted for.</p></div><div><span>04</span><strong>Conditions reviewed</strong><p>Weather and safety triggers have been checked.</p></div></div>{shot('Ground release checklist tied to a team and task')}</div></div></section><section class="section safety-weather"><div class="wrap safety-weather-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">Weather as a safety input</span><h2>A forecast matters when it changes the work.</h2><p class="lead">Current conditions, the three-day outlook, NWS alerts, and aviation weather can inform a briefing or trigger reassessment. The weather workspace keeps the source information available while Safety records the operational response.</p><a class="text-link" href="weather.html">See the Weather workspace →</a></div>{shot('Weather summary and alert alongside safety review')}</div></section><section class="section alt safety-field"><div class="wrap safety-field-grid">{shot('Field hazard report with location and assignment','Mobile companion app')}<div><span class="eyebrow">The field changes the picture</span><h2>Make it easy to report what was found.</h2><p class="lead">The companion app lets a team flag a new hazard from its assignment. The report can update the map and reach Safety, Operations, and Command in time to affect other work.</p><div class="safety-flow"><a href="field.html">Field report</a><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><a href="mapping.html">Map context</a><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><a href="operations.html">Task review</a></div></div></div></section><section class="section safety-medical"><div class="wrap safety-medical-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">Medical support</span><h2>Keep aid resources in the operational picture.</h2><p class="lead">Safety planning includes where medical help is available, how it can be requested, and what conditions affect responders. Resource requests and significant field updates flow through Logistics and the incident record.</p><p>That connection helps command staff see the support available to teams without turning the safety workspace into a patient chart.</p><div class="related"><a href="logistics.html">Logistics &amp; requests →</a><a href="communications.html">Communications →</a><a href="documentation.html">Incident documentation →</a></div></div>{shot('Safety support view with medical resources and requests')}</div></section><section class="section dark safety-connected"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">One shared picture</span><h2>A safety update has a path through the incident.</h2><div class="safety-handoff"><a href="field.html"><strong>Field team</strong><span>Reports a hazard or changing condition.</span></a><a href="mapping.html"><strong>GIS &amp; Safety</strong><span>Locate and assess what is affected.</span></a><a href="operations.html"><strong>Operations</strong><span>Update the task, route, or briefing.</span></a><a href="command.html"><strong>Command</strong><span>Review the wider effect on objectives and resources.</span></a></div></div></section></main>'''
a=s.index('<main>');b=s.index('</main>',a)+len('</main>');s=s[:a]+main+s[b:]
s=s.replace('<title>Medical &amp; Safety | ArcHorizon</title>','<title>Safety &amp; Medical Support | ArcHorizon</title>')
s=s.replace('content="Hazards, operational risk, release checks, and medical support context."','content="ArcHorizon connects hazards, operational risk assessment, ground release checks, weather, medical support, and field safety reports."')
f.write_text(s)
for f in P.glob('*.html'):
 if f.name=='medical-safety.html':continue
 s=f.read_text().replace('>Medical &amp; safety<','>Safety &amp; medical support<').replace('>Medical & safety<','>Safety &amp; medical support<').replace('>Medical &amp; Safety<','>Safety &amp; Medical Support<')
 f.write_text(s)
